{'event': '[CALLBACK] Tool returned a string // Raw response: {"success": "INDUSTRIAL PROCESS DESCRIPTION: Selective Reduction of Ethyl 2-Acetylhexanoate\\n\\n**REACTION OVERVIEW:**\\nThis is a selective ketone reduction where the acetyl group (C(=O)C) of ethyl 2-acetylhexanoate is reduced to a secondary alcohol (CHOH-CH3), while the ester functional group (C(=O)OCC) is preserved. This is a challenging selective reduction requiring careful control.\\n\\n**CATALYTIC SYSTEM:**\\n- Catalyst: Typically transition metal-based (likely Ru, Pd, or Ni catalyst systems)\\n- Raney Nickel or supported Pd/C catalysts are common for ester-compatible ketone reductions\\n- Catalyst loading: 1-10 wt% depending on desired selectivity and reaction rate\\n\\n**REACTION CONDITIONS:**\\n- Temperature: 50-100\\u00b0C (moderate; higher temps risk ester hydrolysis or over-reduction)\\n- Pressure: 20-50 bar H\\u2082 (moderate hydrogen pressure to prevent side reactions)\\n- Solvent: Ethanol, methanol, or mixed alcoholic solvents (polar, hydrogen source compatible)\\n- Duration: 4-12 hours depending on catalyst activity\\n\\n**KEY PROCESS PARAMETERS:**\\n- H\\u2082 stoichiometry: Approximately 1 equiv based on ketone reduction\\n- Selectivity challenge: Must avoid ester reduction (requires milder conditions than ketone reduction)\\n- Temperature control critical: Excessive heat promotes ester hydrolysis or reduction\\n\\n**SELECTIVITY STRATEGY:**\\n- Lower temperature operation favors ketone over ester reduction (kinetic control)\\n- Choice of catalyst crucial\\u2014some catalysts (e.g., certain Pd formulations) show inherent ketone selectivity\\n- Hydrogen pressure moderation prevents over-aggressive reduction\\n\\n**INDUSTRIAL EQUIPMENT:**\\n- High-pressure hydrogenation autoclave/reactor\\n- Hydrogen gas delivery system with pressure regulation\\n- Temperature control jacket\\n- Magnetic stirrer for efficient contact\\n- Gas inlet/outlet with safety relief\\n\\n**WORKUP PROCEDURE:**\\n- Hydrogen venting and depressurization\\n- Catalyst filtration (if heterogeneous)\\n- Solvent removal under reduced pressure\\n- Product purification via distillation or chromatography\\n\\n**SAFETY & ENVIRONMENTAL CONSIDERATIONS:**\\n- Hydrogen handling requires explosion-proof equipment and proper ventilation\\n- Pressure relief and monitoring systems mandatory\\n- Disposal of catalyst residues (heavy metal concerns)\\n- Typical atom economy moderate; minimal waste if selective\\n\\n**SCALE-UP CONSIDERATIONS:**\\n- Heat transfer management essential at scale (exothermic hydrogenation)\\n- Hydrogen mixing/mass transfer may limit throughput at larger scales\\n- Catalyst performance reproducibility critical for consistent selectivity\\n- Continuous flow hydrogenation preferred industrially for better control\\n\\n**ALTERNATIVE APPROACHES:**\\n- Transfer hydrogenation using formic acid/triethylamine (milder, more selective)\\n- Borane-based reductions (NaBH\\u2084 with additives) though less practical at scale\\n- Enzymatic reduction (emerging but not yet commercially mature for this substrate)"}', 'level': 'warning', 'logger': 'lciBuilder.CALLBACKS', 'timestamp': '2026-11-02 14:28:10'}
